}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes an energy-limited progression system: at level 113 there is no extra energy, and progressing further is said to require a purchase. The purchase requirement makes advancement contingent on spending, which supports Pay to Progress. The review does not describe timers, daily rewards, advertisements, or difficulty leaving after investment.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "to have any change at progressing you need to purchase",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall indicator and canonical definition: progress is contingent on making a payment.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer states that purchasing is necessary to have any chance of progressing, making payment a requirement for advancement."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an energy-gated progression system: after the player consumes available energy, they cannot meaningfully progress until the energy regenerates, and the recovery timer is long. This is an in-game timer that imposes a wait before continued play, fitting Playing by Appointment. The requests for a console version, a storyline, bigger maps, and more character customization are hypothetical additions and are not coded. The statement that there is not much to do is a general evaluation rather than a specific supported mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "once you run out you can't really progress any further, and the timer is fairly long to recover fully",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play: patterns that impose arbitrary wait times on players through in-game timers.",
      "rationale": "Progress is blocked after energy is depleted until a long in-game recovery timer expires."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes the game forcing the player to grant access to their contact list, apparently through a notification or permission prompt. This is an in-game request to access the player's social graph, which matches the Friend Spam / Impersonation indicator for using access to a contact list. The review does not explicitly say that messages were actually sent to contacts, so the label rests on the access behavior rather than confirmed spam transmission. The statement that the app is a spam app is evaluative and does not add another label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_FriendSpamImpersonation",
      "span": "Forced notifications to give you permission to my contact list",
      "rule_applied": "Game uses access to the player’s contact list / social graph [indicators]",
      "rationale": "The game is described as forcing the player to grant access to their contact list, which is access to the player's social graph."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes progress being blocked by very difficult levels and tasks. The phrase \"unless you buy boosters\" explicitly makes payment for boosters the route past otherwise impossible tasks, so this is a pay-to-progress mechanic. The statement that hard levels take a week does not by itself establish repetitive or tedious task completion, a fixed play schedule, or an impossible-to-complete game structure, so no temporal label is assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "there are way too many impossible tasks unless you buy boosters",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The review states that progressing past otherwise impossible tasks depends on buying boosters."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a time-limited event with a reward of 525 dice and a progress goal that was nearly complete. The reviewer ran out of dice while only three steps remained, saw a displayed two-day timer, and then found after sleeping that the event had already changed. This means the game ties access to the reward to its own event schedule and pressures the player to keep playing before the event rotates, creating a fear of missing the reward. The review does not explicitly describe paying to replenish dice, daily rewards, repetitive grinding, or a technical fault.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "it said 2Days left. Now just logged in after sleeping and the event is now changed.",
      "rule_applied": "definition; the game requires progress to be completed according to a game-defined event schedule rather than the player's preferred timing.",
      "rationale": "The event's availability is controlled by the game's schedule, so stopping to sleep results in the event being replaced."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_FearOfMissingOutFOMO",
      "span": "The latest event I was about to get 525 dice as reward, I had 3 left to go and I ran out of dice, it said 2Days left. Now just logged in after sleeping and the event is now changed.",
      "rule_applied": "definition; players are pressured by the risk of missing an important event reward if they stop playing.",
      "rationale": "The nearly completed 525-dice reward disappeared when the reviewer stopped playing, exemplifying fear of missing out on a limited event reward."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a friend-invitation reward mechanic: the player invites friends and gets them to join Facebook in exchange for rewards, although the rewards have stopped appearing after the new season began. It also describes luck-dependent gameplay outcomes, including betting dice for possible shutdowns or bank heists and receiving duplicate stickers, indicating unpredictable reward results. The inability to log in is explicitly attributed to an internet connection error, so it is a technical fault and does not support a dark-pattern label. The review does not explicitly describe repetitive tasks, real-money spending, daily rewards, collection completion pressure, or competition. No external search was needed.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_ForcedFellowship",
      "span": "no rewards for inviting friends and getting them to join facebook",
      "rule_applied": "Social Pyramid Scheme indicator",
      "rationale": "The game uses rewards to motivate the player to bring friends into a connected social activity."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "when I finally had more than 500 dices and played max bet, absolutely NO LUCK! No Shut downs and no bank heists. Stickers are duplicates all the way...",
      "rule_applied": "Variable Rewards indicator",
      "rationale": "The span describes luck-dependent, unpredictable outcomes for dice-based events and sticker rewards, including repeated duplicate results."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a current interface behavior in which opening the game or entering 1v1 automatically scrolls to and selects the highest-stakes table available from the player's coin balance. This steers the player toward the most expensive wagering option and makes an unintended bet possible. The reviewer also says that losing the coins leaves players needing to purchase replacement coins, which describes real-money acquisition of in-game currency. The requested lowest-table default is only a suggestion and is not coded.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "Every time you open the game or go to the 1v1 section, it automatically scrolls and selects the highest possible bet table based on your coins.",
      "rule_applied": "Definition: the user interface contains elements designed to focus attention on a specific option or away from safer opt-out options.",
      "rationale": "Automatically steering players to the highest possible bet table directs attention and selection toward the costly option."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "players lose all their hard-earned coins and are forced to buy real ones.",
      "rule_applied": "Definition: players use real-world money to acquire in-game currency for use within the game.",
      "rationale": "The span contrasts earned in-game coins with purchasing replacement coins, implying real-money acquisition of the game's currency."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a base-building game with a grind-oriented progression loop, explicitly calling it a \"grinder.\" It also describes an event presented as a competition, where an ordinary player allegedly has no realistic chance to win. The reviewer attributes success in that event to spending rather than skill, explicitly calling the game pay-to-win and reporting a total spend of 30k to win the event. The review does not describe gambling, purchase-interface friction, recurring fees, daily rewards, or a specific time-based appointment mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "Same old same old grinder",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly characterizes the game's progression as a grinder, describing repetitive gameplay as its defining structure."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "there is absolutely no opportunity for a general player to win an event",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The span describes an in-game event structured around players competing to win."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "Zero skill pay to win",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator: patterns that grant advantages to players who spend money",
      "rationale": "The review states that spending money, rather than skill, provides the advantage needed to win."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes several current game mechanics. Progression is framed as pay-to-win, meaning spending money provides gameplay advantages. Base updates use increasingly long timers, imposing waits before progression can continue. The reviewer also describes endlessly scaling progression and continuing grind with no meaningful endpoint, which indicates an infinite treadmill and grinding. The interface is said to be deliberately confusing and overwhelming in order to push purchases, supporting UI misdirection. Finally, alliance membership is practically compelled because players outside alliances are attacked and bullied; the alliance system also creates hostile interaction between players or groups, supporting forced fellowship and competition. No external search was needed because the mechanics are clear from the review.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "set up in a pay to win way",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator under M_PayToProgress",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly describes a design where spending money grants gameplay advantages."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "the update times for your base are becoming ridiculous",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play indicator: in-game timers that impose arbitrary waits",
      "rationale": "Base updates are governed by increasingly long timers that delay continued progression."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_InfiniteTreadmill",
      "span": "Everything scales endlessly, there is kinda no point to look forward to, just more grind.",
      "rule_applied": "definition: patterns that continually expand the game to never allow players to complete",
      "rationale": "The reviewer describes endlessly scaling progression with no meaningful completion point."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "just more grind",
      "rule_applied": "definition: performing repetitive and tedious tasks in order to make progress",
      "rationale": "The reviewer characterizes ongoing progression as grind, implying repetitive effort is required to advance."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "The UI is deliberately confusing and overwhelming to achieve the effect that you need to buy stuff.",
      "rule_applied": "definition: UI elements purposefully designed to manipulate attention or obscure alternatives",
      "rationale": "The interface is explicitly described as deliberately confusing and overwhelming to steer players toward purchases."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_ForcedFellowship",
      "span": "If you are not in one then you get attacked and bullied.",
      "rule_applied": "Social Obligation / Guilds indicator: creating a sense of obligation to participate in an in-game group",
      "rationale": "The threat of being attacked and bullied makes alliance membership practically compulsory."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "the top ones dictating what to develop, what to rush, etc. If you are not in one then you get attacked and bullied.",
      "rule_applied": "definition: patterns that make players compete against each other",
      "rationale": "The alliance system places players and groups in hostile opposition, including attacks against players outside alliances."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a daily-reward system that was changed so players must wager 50,000 in-game currency to complete and collect the daily rewards. It also mentions timed rewards, but does not describe an arbitrary waiting timer or a requirement to play at a particular clock time. The reward requirement is a gate on daily rewards, not evidence of real-money payment, repetitive grinding, or gambling under this codebook because the review does not state that the wager uses real money or that the reward outcome is chance-based.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "I can't get my daily rewards unless I bet 50,000 with out it I can't complete my daily rewards",
      "rule_applied": "Daily log-in/task rewards indicator; definition",
      "rationale": "The game provides daily rewards and requires the player to complete a daily wagering condition to obtain them, penalizing failure to meet that condition by withholding the rewards."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a monetized progression and collection system. It states that winning rewards requires spending hundreds of dollars, which is a pay-to-progress/pay-to-win mechanic. It also describes sticker collections whose final one or two items are effectively withheld unless the player pays a large amount, which both makes collection completion the objective and indicates manufactured scarcity tied to payment. The comments about addiction, hopes, and the coerced five-star rating are evaluations or out-of-game review behavior, not additional in-game mechanics. No external search was needed because the described mechanics are clear from the review.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "if you want to win anything on this game it will cost hundreds of dollars.",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator under M_PayToProgress: a player can spend real money to obtain an advantage or win in the game.",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly says that winning anything requires spending hundreds of dollars."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_CompleteTheCollection",
      "span": "on the stickers you will never get the last one or two",
      "rule_applied": "Definition and indicator for Complete the Collection: the player is compelled to acquire all items in an in-game collection.",
      "rationale": "The sticker system is presented as a collection whose final missing items determine whether the set is complete."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_DeceptiveLuxury",
      "span": "you will never get the last one or two unless you pay a lot of money to get them.",
      "rule_applied": "Rarity Level / manufactured scarcity: in-game items whose scarcity is tied to purchase or high pricing imply the pattern.",
      "rationale": "The game allegedly withholds the final stickers and makes them obtainable only through a large payment, turning artificial scarcity into a reason to spend."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

